// has written Update() before, twice — and the two lines carrying the new idea
// are gaps: the gate condition and the line that stamps the new moment. Those
// two are the whole mechanism; everything around them is 3.1 and 1.3.
//
// Four timing approaches are laid out for comparison (Time.time timestamp,
// countdown decremented by Time.deltaTime, InvokeRepeating, a coroutine loop);
// the student chooses and justifies one. The walkthrough builds the reference
// approach — the Time.time timestamp, the quick reference's "Cooldown" pattern
// row and the field the Unit 3 docs call lastAttackTime — and the Build Lab is
// where a student who chose any of the other three builds theirs.
//
// FACTUAL GROUND TRUTH, do not "fix" these:
//   - Time.deltaTime IS correct inside a countdown timer. This lesson's rule
//     that velocity code takes no deltaTime is about Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity,
//     which is already measured per second. The two rules do not conflict, and
//     the table prices both rows without ranking them.
//   - no "Attack Cooldown" field exists anywhere in the project — every knob in
//     this lesson is one the student adds themselves.
//   - no spikes are referenced anywhere in this lesson.
//
// The old "Read Function-Level Examples" activity (traffic light, lighthouse,
// bakery, stadium, pedestrian crossing) is deleted. Its one load-bearing fact —
// a rate limit remembers WHEN the last one happened, not HOW MANY are owed —
