<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem: An Action That Happens Too Often</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Rate limiting</strong> is not a game problem. A login form must not accept a
        thousand attempts a second; a printer must not accept a thousand jobs; a turret must not
        fire a thousand bullets. The shape of the solution is the same everywhere —
        <em>remember when the last one happened, and refuse until enough time has passed</em> —
        and this course handed you every part of it in <a href="1.3.html">1.3</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Press Play and stand inside a turret's radius for five seconds, but watch the Console
        instead of the screen. Count the lines. That number is not something you wrote: it is how
        often <code>Update()</code> ran. <strong>There is no way to make <code>Update()</code>
        wait.</strong> Any pause you write has to be a test that fails for a while, not a stop —
        and building that test is the whole of today.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Where this sits in your existing design.</strong> In <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>
        you made detection a <em>condition</em> that is re-evaluated every frame, not an event —
        and you were told that would matter. Here it is. A condition that is true for sixty
        consecutive frames runs whatever is inside it sixty times. Today's field is the memory
        that turns that condition into an event.
    </div>
</div>

