<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs: Four Ways to Time an Action</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        The engine gives you more than one way to remember when something last happened. Read all
        four rows, including the last column — the column nobody reads is the one that decides
        this for you. Then choose, and be ready to defend the choice.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Approach</th><th>What you store</th><th>The gate</th><th>What it costs you</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(a) A <code>Time.time</code> timestamp</strong></td>
            <td>One float: the moment the last shot happened.</td>
            <td><code>Time.time &gt;= lastShot + cooldown</code></td>
            <td>One line of state. Forgetting to write the new timestamp turns the gate permanently true — you get the frame-rate bug straight back. A timestamp is only meaningful against the same clock, so anything that pauses or resets time needs thought. Note also what it does <em>not</em> store: it remembers <strong>when</strong> the last one happened, not <strong>how many</strong> are owed. A second press inside the window is discarded, not queued.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(b) A countdown timer</strong></td>
            <td>One float: seconds <em>remaining</em> until the next shot is allowed.</td>
            <td><code>timer &lt;= 0f</code>, then <code>timer = cooldown</code></td>
            <td>The timer must be decremented by <code>Time.deltaTime</code> on every frame, including frames where the turret is not firing. Forget that and the number quietly stops meaning anything. In exchange, the field reads as "seconds until I can shoot" — which is exactly what a designer wants to see.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(c) <code>InvokeRepeating</code></strong></td>
            <td>Nothing at all — the engine holds the schedule.</td>
            <td><code>InvokeRepeating(nameof(Fire), 0f, cooldown)</code></td>
            <td>Almost no code, and that is the trap: the engine keeps calling your method on cadence whether or not the player is in range, so you still need a range check inside the method. Cancelling is by method <em>name</em>, so renaming the method breaks the cancel silently. Re-tuning the rate at runtime means cancelling and re-invoking.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(d) A coroutine loop</strong></td>
            <td>A running coroutine handle.</td>
            <td><code>while (true) { yield return new WaitForSeconds(t); }</code></td>
            <td>The most powerful and the most readable for <em>sequences</em>, and the only one that expresses "charge, then burst, then rest" cleanly. It costs you a <code>StartCoroutine</code> call, a stored handle to stop it, and a second mental model of when your code runs. The <code>yield</code> is what makes it legal: it hands the frame back to the engine instead of holding it.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>These are not ranked — they are priced.</strong> Every row above is used in real
        shipped games. The question is never "which is correct", it is "which cost am I willing
        to pay for this behaviour, in this project, this week". Row (a) is the cheapest thing
        that works. Row (d) is the right tool the moment a single cooldown is not enough to
        describe the behaviour. Rows (b) and (c) sit between them.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Choosing a Timing Model</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Trace your own 3.2 build. If the player stood inside the radius for one second at 60 frames per second, how many projectile clones did <code>Instantiate</code> create — and which line of your code is responsible for that number?</li>
            <li>Why can't you call a "wait one second" function inside <code>Update()</code>? Describe what the player would see on screen during that wait, and explain why a game engine refuses to let you block the frame.</li>
            <li>Row (a) stores a <em>moment</em>; row (b) stores a <em>duration</em>. Which one can you print in the Console and have a player immediately understand, and which one is easier to compare against a designer's tuning value?</li>
            <li>Read the docs for <code>InvokeRepeating</code>. It has no idea where the player is. What has to move inside your firing method if you choose row (c), and what happens if you forget?</li>
            <li>Row (d) is described as the tool for sequences rather than single actions. What behaviour in this game is a <em>sequence</em> rather than a single action — and which approach do you think the boss in Unit 8 will need?</li>
            <li>Which of the four approaches lets a designer change the fire rate from the Inspector without touching code? Which of them makes that rate hard to change while the game is running?</li>
            <li>Your knob is <code>2</code> and your timestamp field was stamped at <code>12</code>. What is the earliest value of <code>Time.time</code> at which the gate opens?</li>
            <li>Row (a) compares against <code>Time.time</code>; row (b) subtracts <code>Time.deltaTime</code> on every frame. Both measure a gap. Which of the two is tied to how often the frame runs — and which one reports the same wall-clock gap when the frame rate changes?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

